  "pitfalls": [
    "OEE, availability, performance, yield and rejection rate must be recomputed from totals for every period, machine and shift; averaging row-level percentages overweights short runs.",
    "Time units must match: planned time, run time, downtime and ideal cycle time all need the same unit, or OEE and performance are badly wrong.",
    "Definitions differ between plants (are breaks, changeovers and planned maintenance downtime?), so compare OEE only within one definition.",
    "Product mix drives yield and speed: a line or shift that ran harder parts looks worse without being worse (Simpson's paradox). Compare within a product or family.",
    "Machines, shifts and products with only a few runs swing wildly; treat rates from fewer than about 20 runs as noise.",
    "Good counts that include reworked units overstate first pass yield and OEE quality."
  ]
